<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">ARCHITECTURE</p><h1>Follow the request.<br>See every boundary.</h1><p>Nora owns its framework core and public APIs. Composer packages may be used where useful, but Laravel and Symfony are not underlying frameworks.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="architecture-flow" aria-label="HTTP request lifecycle">
    <div><b>01</b><strong>Bootstrap</strong><span>Environment + PHP config</span></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><b>02</b><strong>Request</strong><span>Global input boundary</span></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><b>03</b><strong>Middleware</strong><span>Ordered pipeline</span></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><b>04</b><strong>Router</strong><span>Method + path match</span></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><b>05</b><strong>Handler</strong><span>Application response</span></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><b>06</b><strong>Response</strong><span>Headers + body</span></div>
</div><div class="article-layout architecture-notes"><article class="prose"><h2>One application, explicit seams</h2><p><code>ApplicationBootstrap</code> loads environment and configuration, creates the application, and registers the route file. Requests pass through middleware before router dispatch. The exception handler centralizes uncaught failures and uses production-safe responses by default.</p><p>Controllers and application code return <code>Response</code> objects. The view renderer escapes interpolated values; it does not evaluate arbitrary PHP templates or provide a raw HTML escape hatch.</p><h2>Composable subsystems</h2><p>Configuration, validation, PDO helpers, migrations, sessions, CSRF, structured logging, and the CLI live in Nora-owned components. The optional reactive engine adds one mounted server-rendered component per page, with state kept in the session and named actions checked on the server.</p><h2>Known boundaries</h2><p>The development PHP server is not a production deployment strategy. Request bodies are read before all application-level limits can be enforced; production ingress must impose request-size and timeout limits. The reactive API is experimental and does not provide nested components, shared state, reactive forms, or navigation.</p></article><aside class="article-aside"><strong>Core dependencies</strong><span>PHP 8.2+</span><span>PDO for database drivers</span><span>No Laravel or Symfony runtime</span><hr><strong>Optional integration</strong><span>AI provider-agnostic by design</span><span>Not required by core</span></aside></div></div></section>
